  **11. Witness chain correctness detail**: The chain: for each peak i: if within reach of last kept → if survivor keep & update last; else cand = i (the FARTHEST sub-threshold within reach — since iterating in m/z order, cand gets overwritten by later peaks, so cand = last (farthest) sub-threshold within reach). When out of reach: push cand as witness, last=cand, re-test reach from cand. If still out of reach → push i regardless (survivor or not!). Note: `keep.push_back(i); last = i;` — this pushes a sub-threshold peak that's out of reach of even the candidate — "the far side of a gap nothing bridges". Wait, can that happen? If x (current peak) is out of reach of cand (farthest within reach of last)... then gap between last kept and x can't be bridged? Hmm — actually consider: last=100, cand=100.5 (within reach), x=101.2 out of reach of cand(100.5) if hop*100.5 < 0.7? hop=3e-4 → reach ≈ +0.03. So x=101.2 out of reach. Push cand, then x still out of reach → push x. But could an intermediate chain... cand was the farthest within reach of last; between cand and x there are no other peaks (x is next). So minimal. But pushing x when sub-threshold: is that needed? The gap from cand to x exceeds hop — cannot be bridged (no peaks in between). For band-spectrum existence, we need every band range containing a peak to contain a kept one. If x sub-threshold and we DIDN'T keep it: band range containing x but not cand or next kept... x is the first peak after a gap; the band containing x — does it contain another kept peak? Not necessarily! So x must be kept. That's the "first/last peak" anchor generalization. Correct design.

