  Now — **the strongest candidate bugs to report**:

  (A1) **GCC `#pragma GCC optimize("fp-contract=off")` caveat**: GCC documents the optimize attribute/pragma as debugging-only; historically it does NOT reliably apply -f options per-function in all versions, and there are reports it can reset other flags (e.g. drop command-line -O level or -march for those functions, or fail to disable contraction). The defense-in-depth (volatile) does cover the one a*b+c site, so bit-identity likely holds anyway — but then the pragma + CMake flag are belt on top of the volatile; the risk is the pragma miscompiling or silently not doing anything while giving false confidence. Recommend: rely on volatile (provably sufficient), or verify on GCC x86-64 with -march=native (FMA) without the CMake flag. Actually — wait, there's a subtlety: with `#pragma GCC optimize` anything, GCC may compile those functions at a DIFFERENT optimization level? The pragma without an -O option keeps command-line -O. I believe options accumulate. But there IS a known GCC bug where __attribute__((optimize)) disables inlining into callers or mis-applies. I'll report it as a portability risk with the concrete failure mode and recommend a CI configuration (Linux GCC -O3 -march=x86-64-v3, no CMake flag) to prove the volatile alone suffices — the test claims to pin D's boxes and "fails with the guards removed" (on Apple clang arm64 presumably), but GCC+x86 FMA is a different contraction path.

  Hmm, actually another genuine FP issue: **`std::sqrt` and contraction aside, x87 excess precision?** x86-64 uses SSE2 — fine. 32-bit x87 would be a hazard but C++20 macOS/Linux 64-bit only. Skip.

  (A2) **from_chars float on libstdc++/libc++ availability**: `#if defined(__cpp_lib_to_chars)` — this macro is defined when integer from_chars exists (C++17), NOT float! __cpp_lib_to_chars covers integer to_chars/from_chars since C++17; floating-point from_chars has its own macro `__cpp_lib_to_chars` bumped value 201611L vs... Actually: __cpp_lib_to_chars 201611L = integer only; the float support uses the SAME macro name with a later value? Let me recall: P0068? Floating-point from_chars is P0067 (same paper as integer). libstdc++ implemented float from_chars in GCC 11; the macro __cpp_lib_to_chars remained 201611L until... GCC bumped __cpp_lib_to_chars to 201611L only. There's no separate macro for FP from_chars! That's the known problem: you cannot detect FP from_chars support via feature-test macro reliably. libc++ defines __cpp_lib_to_chars 201611L with integer-only for a long time — that's exactly why the code has the `#else` branch for libc++. But the test is `#if defined(__cpp_lib_to_chars)` — libc++ ALSO defines __cpp_lib_to_chars (201611L, integer-only)! So on macOS/libc++, __cpp_lib_to_chars IS defined (integer from_chars exists), and the code would take the from_chars path with `std::from_chars(..., double&)` — which on older libc++ (before macOS 26 availability-gated FP from_chars) **does not compile** (no FP overload) — a build break, or on newer libc++ it's availability-gated → runtime availability issue/compile error depending on deployment target. Wait, the code comment says "libc++ does not define __cpp_lib_to_chars" — that claim is WRONG. libc++ does define __cpp_lib_to_chars (201611L) since it has integer to_chars. Hmm — does it? libc++ has <charconv> integer since LLVM 7, and defines __cpp_lib_to_chars 201611L. Yes it does. The header includes <version> which provides the macro. So on macOS, `#if defined(__cpp_lib_to_chars)` is TRUE, the istringstream fallback is dead code, and `std::from_chars` for double on libc++ < LLVM 18(?) → compile error.